"Mistral raises €3B to make sovereign, open-weight AI the technology frontier European AI startup Mistral has raised a €3 billion Series D funding round at a valuation of over €21 billion. Mistral secured €3 billion in Series D funding led by Samsung Electronics—the largest ever for a European tech company—achieving a post-money valuation exceeding €21 billion. The secured funds will be allocated toward scaling frontier AI research, expanding compute capacity to train more powerful models, and broadening its global commercial presence. Through a full-stack approach encompassing open-weight models and infrastructure, it provides enterprises and public institutions with a sovereign AI environment free from vendor lock-in. Hallo, Deutschland! French AI company Mistral has established a new hub in Munich, Germany—Europe's largest industrial powerhouse—to aggressively target the industrial and physics AI sectors. Mistral opened the Munich hub and deployed dedicated research teams for Physics AI and industrial AI to support key German industries, including automotive, energy, and manufacturing. Mistral is collaborating with BMW on crash simulations and with Siemens Energy on industrial AI applications, while absorbing physics specialists from Emmi AI, which it acquired in May 2026. To secure Europe's AI sovereignty, Mistral plans to build 1 GW of proprietary European computing capacity by 2030, offering data security and auditability within customer infrastructure through open-weight models. Mistral and Mozilla are bringing open, private and multilingual AI to your web browser Mistral and Mozilla have partnered to introduce Mistral's open, multilingual AI models into Firefox Smart Window, Mozilla's web browsing AI assistant. Mozilla's AI browsing assistant, 'Firefox Smart Window (beta),' is powered by Mistral models to support complex search summarization and tab-based information exploration. It is initially available to users in France and North America, with plans to expand support to the United Kingdom and Germany later this year. It delivers tailored AI optimized for local languages and cultural contexts, with privacy protections applied by default, such as not saving conversations and adhering to a zero data retention policy. Cloudera and Mistral Partner to Bring Specialized, Sovereign Intelligence to Enterprise Data 미스트랄(Mistral)과 클라우데라(Cloudera)가 기업들이 자체 환경에서 데이터를 완전히 제어하며 맞춤형 및 주권형 AI를 구축할 수 있도록 파트너십을 체결했다. 미스트랄 모델이 클라우데라의 하이브리드 데이터 플랫폼과 통합되어 온프레미스, 퍼블릭/프라이빗 클라우드, 완전 에어갭 환경 등 자체 인프라에서 추론을 실행할 수 있다. 규제 산업군(금융, 제조업, 통신 등) 기업들이 수십 년간 축적된 독점 데이터를 활용해 데이터 소유권을 유지하면서 맞춤형 AI 모델을 학습 및 구축할 수 있도록 지원한다. 데이터, 지능, 컴퓨팅 및 운영을 고객 통제 하에 두어 기업이 '범용 AI 임대'에서 '고유한 지능 소유'로 전환할 수 있는 소버린 AI(Sovereign AI)를 구현한다. Modernizing complex legacy code with AI agents. Introduces a case study and key methodologies for successfully migrating a complex Fortran 77 legacy codebase of 40,000 lines into a modern C++ system using AI agents. Executed a project to convert a 40,000-line physics-based reservoir simulator lacking test suites and centralized documentation into C++. Key success factors identified include establishing an equivalence testing framework to verify numerical consistency, pre-documenting via AI agents, and maintaining a balance between human review and agent autonomy. Going beyond mere syntax translation, refactored the architecture from procedural global memory (COMMON blocks) structures to align with object-oriented C++ and modern scientific computing frameworks such as PETSc. Splice CEO Kakul Srivastava thinks AI emails are killing conversations An interview in which Kakul Srivastava, CEO of music sample platform Splice, expresses concern that AI-generated documents and tools may undermine human-centered genuine communication and creative processes. Splice avoids one-click music generation methods (such as Suno) and aims to introduce AI tools where human creators remain central. CEO Kakul Srivastava pointed out that AI-written documents and emails hinder deep thinking and genuine conversations among team members. Emphasizing the importance of conversations filled with human intent and contemplation in remote-first environments, she noted that the same principle applies to music creation. An OpenAI safety employee has quit and is sounding the alarm An employee who authored safety reports at OpenAI has resigned, criticizing the AI industry's safety culture as fundamentally broken. David Robinson, who wrote safety reports for flagship models at OpenAI, resigned and publicly criticized industry culture in an article for The Atlantic. He argued that Silicon Valley overlooks potential risks while overly focusing on excessive optimism and speed, asserting that AI companies need nuclear power plant-level safeguards and prudence. Major AI companies, including Anthropic and Google DeepMind, have recently seen a series of departures and public warnings from safety-focused researchers. Reverse Item Response Theory for Sparsity-Robust Ranking in Fragmented Cancer Drug-Response Matrices A study on robust ranking prediction applying reverse item response theory to address the missing data issue in cancer cell drug responses. Introduced reverse Item Response Theory (reverse IRT) to pharmacogenomics, conceptualizing cancer types as 'examinees' with resistance capabilities and drugs as 'items' with evasion difficulties. Applied to 242,036 measurements in the GDSC2 database, achieving higher rank recovery (Delta-rho = +0.089 to +0.095) compared to simple averages and an optimal Brier score even under conditions with a 60% missing rate. Demonstrated an 82% directional agreement in cross-validation with the PRISM dataset, validating the methodological robustness of the evaluation in fragmented data environments. How Far is Adam from Natural Gradient Descent? This study analyzes how closely Adam, a standard optimization algorithm in deep learning, geometrically aligns with Natural Gradient Descent (NGD) and identifies the true factors behind its empirical success. The researchers analyzed Adam's full update rule, including momentum, from the perspective of a diagonal empirical Fisher information approximation and measured its geometric deviation from NGD across four loss landscapes. While the deviation was small under well-conditioned settings, it reached approximately 10^3 in ill-conditioned settings and neural networks; however, geometric drift did not adversely affect final loss minimization, even if it slowed initial convergence. The study identified that Adam's superior optimization performance stems not from closely tracking the NGD trajectory, but rather from a balance between structural approximation error and momentum smoothing. FourierQK: Filter Shape, Admissibility and the Leakage-Coverage Law A study analyzing the optimal filter shape and spectral leakage-coverage characteristics of FourierQK, which applies a band-pass filter instead of the Q/K dot product in attention mechanisms. Confirmed that suppression of DC and Nyquist components alongside an oscillatory band-pass structure is essential. Achieved a +1.15 nats performance improvement over standard dot-product attention at a single-scale bandwidth (sigma ~= 2 bins) centered around the paragraph level (~70 tokens). Demonstrated that an admissible filter (Mexican Hat DOG m = 2) outperforms Gaussian filters and partially prevents two-sided FFT leakage. Fast Polynomial Transcendentals for LLMs This study improves LLM training speed by applying polynomial approximation operations to resolve bottlenecks in special function computations on modern GPUs such as NVIDIA Blackwell. To reduce bottlenecks in Special Function Units (SFUs) resulting from GPU architecture evolution, native PyTorch operations were replaced with FMA-based 3rd to 4th-degree polynomial programs. Across four integration workloads in a GB200 environment (dense SiLU, tanh-softcapped attention, sigmoid attention, and routed-expert SwiGLU), overall training throughput improved from 0.3% up to 8.0%. At the point of training on approximately 100 billion tokens, the difference in final smoothed training loss compared to existing operations remained between -0.107 and +0.079, stably preserving model performance. SW-KAN: Kolmogorov-Arnold Networks with Stieltjes-Wigert q-Orthogonal Polynomials This study proposes SW-KAN, a novel Kolmogorov-Arnold Network architecture incorporating Stieltjes-Wigert q-orthogonal polynomials to resolve the domain mismatch between unbounded real inputs and orthogonal polynomial bases. It adopts Stieltjes-Wigert q-orthogonal polynomials defined on the semi-infinite interval (0, infinity) and utilizes a smooth exponential-of-tanh mapping to reliably bridge the domain gap while maintaining favorable gradient conditions. Computational efficiency is achieved by utilizing a numerically stable three-term recurrence relation that evaluates polynomial expansions in O(N) operations without invoking special functions. By introducing inductive bias through a log-normal weight structure and learnable q-parameters, it demonstrates superior expressiveness and accuracy-efficiency trade-offs over conventional polynomial KANs, even in resource-constrained settings such as reduced dimensionality or limited data. The Four Horsemen of Agentic Coding An article analyzing the adverse side effects lying behind the usefulness of agent-based coding: slop, alienation from work, deskilling of development, and the erosion of social bonds within teams. Due to the distinct style generated by LLMs (slop), code that humans are reluctant to read is increasing, posing a risk of turning codebases into wastelands. The approach of directing and reviewing agents rather than coding directly increases the psychological distance from one's work, undermining ownership, craftsmanship, and love for software. Dependence on AI tools reduces incentives to maintain and acquire new development skills, while diminishing opportunities for questions and mutual recognition among peers, thereby damaging team cohesion. Show GN: ALPS Writer · ADR Writer - Product Specification and Architectural Decision Management Plugins for Claude Code and Codex ALPS Writer and ADR Writer, open-source plugins designed to help systematically document and manage Product Requirement Documents (PRDs) and Architectural Decision Records (ADRs) within Claude Code and Codex environments, have been released. ALPS Writer drafts Product Requirement Documents (PRDs) through a Q&A format, supporting both Full and a 4-section Lite format. ADR Writer records the rationale and constraints behind architectural decisions to support implementation, code review, and consistency verification between documentation and code. Even if the plugins are removed, generated documents and code are preserved in the repository; they run in Claude Code and Codex environments and require Node.js 24 or higher. ds4 Expands from DeepSeek-Exclusive to a Local Inference Engine Supporting Qwen and GLM C-based local inference engine DwarfStar 4 (ds4) expands support beyond DeepSeek to include Qwen and GLM models, delivering a high-performance local inference and coding agent integration environment. An MIT-licensed inference engine supporting Mac, CUDA, and ROCm environments, applying asymmetric 2-bit quantization technology to efficiently run large-scale MoE models on local machines Three interfaces—CLI, local HTTP API, and native coding agent—share model state and cache, while SSD-based long prefix caching optimizes the prefill process upon restart Provides OpenAI and Anthropic-style API compatibility, enabling simple connection of various coding agents such as Codex, Claude Code, and OpenCode to a local server What’s next after Jev? Metacache: Reasoning by Construction [D] This post introduces Metacache, an inference framework that shifts away from a single monolithic model approach toward constructing and reusing compound systems combining small agents with code orchestration. To address the unpredictability and difficulty of modifying monolithic models, the paradigm is shifting toward compound systems combining small agents with code-based orchestration layers. Instead of having a model answer directly, it proposes a method where the model constructs and executes a compound system (reasonlet) composed of small agents, returning both the result and the system itself. The returned reasonlet can be saved locally for repeated execution and modification without re-invoking the model, or cached on the provider side (metacache) to save computational resources. iPhone 18 Pro Max can’t call or text on AT&T? Apple will replace it for free Apple is offering free device replacements as some iPhone 18 Pro Max units on the AT&T network experience dropped calls and lost data connections. Issues have been reported where some iPhone 18 Pro Max devices on the AT&T network lose cellular service, leaving them unable to make calls, send texts (including to 911), or use mobile data. As a preventative measure, Apple released iOS 27.0.1 and a carrier settings update; however, devices already experiencing connectivity loss cannot be recovered via software, so Apple is providing free hardware replacements. While the exact cause has not been officially announced, teardown analyses by iFixit and TechInsights suggest a potential link to cellular hardware components, such as the new millimeter wave (mmWave) antenna. Presentation: Building GenAI Platform at DoorDash Presents the architectural journey and engineering strategy of DoorDash building and evolving an internal generative AI platform to support over 5,000 internal users. Shifted from a commercial vendor model-centric setup to open-weights models and introduced an LLM and agent gateway. Scaled the platform foundation while balancing accuracy, latency, and cost. Starting with an OpenAI contract signed in April 2023, evolved the system from simple model usage to workflows and further into AI agents. Cute Characters Masking 'AI Backlash'… Alexandr Wang Leads 'Muse' to Box-Office Hit Big tech companies are actively adopting friendly mascots and public-friendly marketing strategies to ease public aversion and anxiety toward AI and expand their user base. Under the leadership of 29-year-old CAIO Alexandr Wang, Meta propelled its AI personal assistant app 'Muse' to No. 1 on the App Store with its mascot 'Jolly' and unconventional meme marketing. Major AI companies such as OpenAI ('Dots') and Anthropic ('Claude') are also utilizing character mascots and merchandise to reduce technological aversion and build a friendly image. Rather than getting caught up in technology races or regulatory debates, Meta is focusing on securing mainstream real-world users through everyday shopping and payment partnerships alongside an intuitive user experience (UX). Bubbleshare Unveils AI Search Performance Analytics Solution... \"Accurate Citations Matter More Than Simple Exposure\" Bubbleshare has unveiled 'VIVI,' an AI search visibility solution designed to go beyond mere exposure by supporting accurate brand citations within AI responses and optimizing content tailored to each stage of the buyer's journey. The solution 'VIVI' evaluates brand and competitor mentions and citations across major AI search environments like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Perplexity in four distinct stages: existence, exposure opportunity, actual mention, and source citation. Moving beyond tracking simple average exposure rates, it designs question sets aligned with customer intent and the buyer's journey—such as information exploration, alternative comparison, and purchase decisions—to conduct in-depth analysis. After analyzing low-performing prompts and content gaps, it provides integrated support ranging from specific content improvement drafts to actionable marketing execution measures. Build 'Your Own Claude Code'... Anthropic Launches Custom Extension Feature 'Mods' Anthropic has launched 'Mods', an extension feature for Claude Code that allows developers to customize and share the UI and behavior directly. Launch of 'Mods', a plugin extension feature that allows users to modify Claude Code's UI and behavior using JavaScript or TypeScript Installable and shareable via the '/plugin' command in the CLI and desktop apps, supported from Claude Code version 2.1.287 and above Runs with user permissions without a separate sandbox, posing security risks, so safety measures such as verification commands and the '--safe-mode' flag are provided Anthropic Launches 'Claude Frontier Academy' with $100 Million Investment to Train 10,000 FDEs Anthropic has launched the $100 million 'Claude Frontier Academy' to train 10,000 forward-deployed engineers to lead enterprise AI implementations. Anthropic invests $100 million to launch the 'Claude Frontier Academy' to train 10,000 enterprise and partner engineers as Forward Deployed Engineers (FDEs) by the end of 2027 Modeled after medical residency programs, the training offers in-person practical education followed by a 12-week 'FDE Residency' executing Claude projects at participant companies before granting certification Major global enterprises including Accenture, Deloitte, McKinsey, and Morgan Stanley participate in the inaugural cohort, accelerating efforts to capture the enterprise AI implementation market Apple Tightens macOS 'Full Disk Access' Controls Amid Rise in Unauthorized AI Agent Access Apple is strengthening 'Full Disk Access' permission controls in macOS to mitigate privacy breach risks arising from desktop AI agents accessing user data. Apple announced the introduction of additional control mechanisms, including explicit approval procedures, to macOS 'Full Disk Access' permissions to prevent unauthorized data access and security risks posed by AI agents. Pointing out that broad permissions originally designed for backup purposes could recently be exploited by AI agents or potentially expose the private information of conversation partners, Apple has moved to preemptively block such risks. This move comes in response to growing concerns over data misuse by desktop AI agents, such as controversy surrounding OpenAI's ChatGPT Messages integration and Meta's AI agent Muse accessing personal information. Chinese AI Penetrates South Korean Enterprises... Warning Lights on 'Model Dependency' As South Korean enterprises increasingly adopt Chinese open-weight AI models such as Alibaba's Qwen, concerns are growing over dependency on specific models and the burden of switching costs due to potential future changes in licensing policies. An increasing number of South Korean enterprises are adopting Chinese open-weight AI models like Alibaba's Qwen on their own on-premise servers due to cost reductions and superior Korean-language performance. While Qwen-based derivative models and downloads on Hugging Face have surged, the issue of 'model lock-in' is being raised, as deeper integration into internal enterprise systems makes it harder to switch to other models later. Risks regarding licensing changes in subsequent versions are emerging, such as recent large flagship models like Qwen 3.8 Max requiring separate commercial licensing agreements for enterprises exceeding a certain revenue threshold. [Marginalized SW Part 1] SW Disappears from Budget Proposal... Concerns Over National Competitiveness While the government significantly increases next year's AI budget, support budgets for the software (SW) industry—which serves as the core foundation for AI—have been reduced, raising concerns over weakened national competitiveness. The Ministry of Science and ICT's 2027 AI budget reached 9.4211 trillion KRW, up 84.3% from the previous year, whereas the non-R&D SW budget dropped by around 40 billion KRW to approximately 290 billion KRW. Budgets for key SW projects, including SW infrastructure expansion, public SaaS development and transition support, and global expansion capability enhancement, were cut significantly, and university support focus is shifting from SW to AI. Industry and academia emphasize that for AI technology to be applied in real-world industrial settings and generate value, support from the traditional SW ecosystem, such as ERP, DB, and embedded SW, is essential. 'AI for Everyone' Transforms Daily Life… What Changes Will It Bring? Introduces real-life AI applications solving everyday problems in areas like shopping, travel, and caregiving, as well as the 'AI Festa 26' event showcasing them and the government's 'AI for Everyone' policy. 'AI Festa 26' will be held from October 6 to 8 at COEX in Seoul, showcasing AI technologies and application cases expanding into everyday life. A wide range of life-oriented services will be featured, including Buzzni's shopping AI, Yanolja's travel solutions, Sigonggan's shopping assistance for the visually impaired, and Zeroweb's senior care. Under the government's 'AI for Everyone' policy, a consortium of SK Telecom, Kakao, and KT plans to launch a public AI service in December following a beta test this month to assist with hospital reservations and public service applications."